INSTALLATION GUIDE
UKUUCP README	2.0	
Sun Mar  3 23:37:27 GMT 1985

Lee McLoughlin  ukc!lmcl  01-435-7141 X 636

This is the standard version of uucp for use by sites in the UK
it is inuse by most of the sites on the UKnet and by many
others.  There have been tons of bug fixes and enhancements
from people on the usenet (thank you).  Speed is substantially
improved.  This version fixes essentially all of the McGeady's bug list.

Enhancements:

Security		Only sites listed in the L.sys file can call in.
			All other sites are rejected.

Networks		Can now be used to transfer over networks that don't
			provide full 8-bit communication, PSS and terminal
			connections onto JANET for example.

Grade Changes		By default uux commands will be queued at priority
			'o'.  uucp commands at 'n'. So user file transfers
			will beat commands.
			 uucico can also be told to do work for JUST a single
			grade (see 12 below).

Dialers			Lots of dialers included.

Subdirectories		/usr/spool/uucp can now be split into 7, or
			more, subdirectories. This is a huge help on
			busy systems.
/usr/lib/uucp/L.dirs	List of existing subdir's used.

/usr/lib/uucp/L.cmds	List of commands permitted for remote execution.
			A line of form 'PATH=...' sets the search path.

Accounting & multi-machine sites
/usr/lib/uucp/L.info	a somewhat more powerfull accounting/authorisation
			system is now installed (warning accounts gatherer
			not yet implemented).  L.cmds can specify wether a
			user is allowed to export the command and L.info
			can be used to specify the level of accounting and
			wether a machine is local or remote.  For remote
			sites an arbitrary site name (not necessarily the
			same as the current machine name) may be given. This
			allows several machine at a site to appear as one 
			machine to the outside world.

expect-send sequence	
			Re-written to be able to cope with networks.
			:string		send string (won't add a \r)
			?string		expect string. If found ignore all
					fields till next :string.
			!string		send only if previous ?string failed.
			Escape characters now permitted: \r, \n.
			\d	pause 1 second (\d\d pauses 2 seconds)
			P_EVEN  send even parity (default).
			P_ZERO  P_ODD, P_ONE other parity modes.
			\05	Send a control-E

binary-only		with a modicum of care uucp will now run binary only
			since it can now read all its configuration information
			from files (including the sitename if necessary).

uusnap			Displays spooled files, and pending uuxqts.


This version runs on all VAXen and PDPs under UNIX-V7, 2.8-2.9 Bsd,
4.1bsd-4.2BSD, System 3-5.2 USG.
It also runs on Gould/SEL Concept series machines (e.g. 32/8750), 
DUAL 68000 unisoft.

UUCP installers should read the two papers (by Dave Nowitz)
in Vol 2B of version 7 manuals (a revised version of the
implement paper is in DOCS/implement) and DOCS/setup.tblms (which is
slightly out of date but still usefull).
Understand each step below before executing.
Some steps will vary slightly from system to system.

A NOTE ON SUBDIRECTORIES:
The mutliple subdirectories are implemented by mapping the original filenames
into pathnames by matching a prefix on the filename against the name of a 
subdirectory of SPOOL.  For example the name D.dukeX5678 would be mapped into
/usr/spool/uucp/D.dukeX/D.dukeX5678.  Because it is just matching on a prefix
it is important to have the prefixes ordered correctly, the most specific ones
come first.  So a prefix of just "D." would have to come after "D.dukeX". 
Otherwise "D." would also match "D.dukeX5678" so the D.dukeX directory would
never be used.
  The advantage of subdirectories is in the reduced directory scanning time.
with, for example, 10 sites each with 200 queued files under the old system
of a single SPOOL directory it would mean scanning the 2000 C. files AND the
2000 D. files in order to find the first LLEN C. files to obey, a total of 4000
files.  By giving each site its own D. and C. directories uucico has to scan
only the 200 C. files for the site its talking to.

**** INSTALLATION *******


1. Editing Makefile and uucp.h
	if you are using SCCS run 'make sources'

	Often used defaults are given in brackets.
	Options marked with an asterisk must either be the same in both uucp.h
	and the Makefile or require similar options to be selected in both.
	NOTE: There are (very) many other defines in both the Makefile and
	uucp.h.  Changing those not listed here should only be done if you
	are sure of what you're doing, and bear in mind that full pathnames
	are often used throughout the system so changing just the prefix in
	one place is often not enough.
Makefile->
DESTDIR=The entire uucp system (including lib) is installed below here.
	(I cannot think of a good reason why any site should need this but
	 the original authors must have. LMCL).
SRCDIR= the location of these sources.  Just used to avoid relative pathnames
	(eg: /usr/src/cmd/UKUUCP/uucp).
OWNER=	the owner the uucp system will run in. (uucp)
GROUP=  the group the uucp system will run in. (uucp)
CHOWN=	the path name of the chown program, to be used by root. (/etc/chown)
CHGRP=	the path name of the chown program, to be used by root. (/etc/chgrp)
RANLIB= the path name of the ranlib program, "echo" if unavailable. (ranlib)
INST=	a program used to install software. (/usr/bin/install)
MYNAME= the name of the local site, used to create the initial directories.
	(*) Must be the same as the MYNAME #define in uucp.h.
LIBDEPS=, LIBNDIR=, LLIBS= 
	(*) In uucp.h #define LIBNDIR if using the first set of Makefile
	   definitions.  If the directory accessing routine are part of the
	   standard library then use the second set of Makefile definitions
	   and #define BSD42 in uucp.h.  If the routines have been installed
	   as /usr/lib/libndir.a and in /usr/include/ndir.h then define NDIR
	   in uucp.h and use the third set of Makefile definitions.
UUCPDIR=  (*)the parent for uucp/ and uucppublic/, usually /usr/spool but quite
	often changed to be whichever filestore has the most free space. E.g:
	west44 uses /f1, ic-cs uses /users.  NOTE no trailing " on define.
LOCN = 	(*)Location. Used to select the sccs id's that are to be used.
	NOSCCS will cause them to be ommited, best if on a small machine.
	ORIGSCCS will use the (now totally out of date) original sccsid
	UKC & MASTER are used by the backbone site ukc.
	anything else will use the UKUUCP sccsid, which is recommended.
	Defining UKC will also effect where logs will be kept, see LOGFILE
	in uucp.h.
FIO =	Defining FIO will allow the use of the 'f' protocol by Piet Beertema.
	This protocol will only work on connections which have flow-control
	working correctly.  JNT-PAD's seem to fail.  (DATAWIDTH MUST also
	be defined so FIO can be disabled on all lines on which it is not
	wanted.  This is done line by appending 'fN' to the
	end of the width entry, without this uucico will fail to connect.) 
	With FIO defined you should also add
		-Dvoid=int
	for systems without the void cast. (Check first to see if none of
	the include file in your system have a similar #define or
	typedef already.)   On Vaxen also add
		-Dvax
	if not already present,  this is to get the correct version of the
	checksum routines.
BIGLDFLAGS= give which ever flags are suitable for large programs.
install: If you are using a PDP or another small machine then it may be usefull
	to make these program "sticky-bitted".  Remove the comment marks 
	on the chmod line in the install option to have this done.

uucp.h->
	Select the method appropriate to your system for finding the sitename.
MYNAME	(*) the same as in Makefile, used in case the above method fails.
ZAP	Avoid unnecessary resends.  Relies on working fseek.  To test fseek
	cc tseek.c;a.out   and see what it advises if it seems to be OK then
	define ZAP.  If not comment it out.  Without it some degree of
	unnecessary resending may occur but the system will still work.
ACCT	define for mcvax style accounting (charge for overheads)
KICKINIT  define if you have a program which can close down getty for dialout
	only if you have a working acu/kickint program see SUPPORT directory.
WS	define when KICKINIT'ing by using Bill Sebok's acu program
DATAWIDTH  define for narrow datapaths, This allows uucp to work over
	X3/X25 networks like JANET and PSS.
FOURBIT SIXBIT SEVENBIT  various versions of narrow channels. FOURBIT
	effectively sends has hex-pairs.  SEVENBIT is designed work over
	reasonably intelligent nextworks.  SIXBIT is a variant of SEVENBIT.
LIBNDIR/NDIR/BSD42 (*) See LIBDEPS under Makefile.
If you have it include <sysexits.h> to use exit codes otherwise use the defines.
Go down through the various devices and comment in the ones you are going to
use.
SYSIII	define if you are on a sys3 compatiable system.
SYS5	define if you are on a sys5 compatiable system.
BAD_PRINTF  on some systems a printf format of "%02d" doesn't print out
	the argument in two characters with leading zeros (cc prtest.c;a.out  to
	find out if you're machine is one) in this case define BAD_PRINTF to
	have it use the "%0.2d" format instead.
OLDTOUCH  define chmod changes the ctime.
WFMASK used for umask call for all uucp work files
LOGMASK	- for LOGFILE, SYSLOG, ERRLOG
BASEMODE final mode given to files at the remote site. Remember if they cannot
	be read by programs at the remote site rnews/rmail (and everyone else)
	will get pretty annoyed.
PRIV_UIDS users with getuid() <= PRIV_UIDS are 'privileged' (100) (Not
	used if SYSINFO defined, which it should be, since the uids are
	read from the L.info file.)
UUCPPEOPLE people entitled to run privileged but who are not in PRIV_UIDS
	(Not used if SYSINFO defined, which it should be, since the uids are
	read from the L.info file.)
	NOTE: For the SUPPORT utility  asuucp (which runs the cron scripts)
	to run correctly the uid that cron runs under must be included in the
	range of either PRIV_UIDS or UUCPPEOPLE
UUCPDIR (*) see UUCPDIR for Makefile.
LOCKDIR This is the directory where lock files will be created.  It must
        be writeably by uucp.  Normally this is UUCPDIR/uucp but many sites
        prefer to change it to be "/tmp so that it will be automatically
	cleaned on system restart.  If the ucu program is to be used then
	"/tmp is a better choice since ordinary users will not have to be
	given write permission on the main uucp directory.
SLCKTIME  system/device timeout (LCK.. files) if you call up sites very often
	then you may wish to reduce this. It is generally not recommended.
LOGFILE ERRLOG	(*) (see also LOCN under Makefile) if UKC is defined
	then the logs are kept in file in /usr/adm/log.  Normally they
	will be kept in the SPOOL directory.
MAIL	you may wish to redefine this if you are running sendmail/mmdf.

Once these have been done you should not have to edit any of the other
makefiles or shell scripts since the 'make depend' option will do that for
you.

2. Make the new commands.
	Firstly if you wish to run with the KICKINIT code then look at the
	two version of acu (v7acu.c and BSD42acu.c) in the SUPPORT directory.
	Whichever version you are going to run rename as acu.c.  If you are
	not going to use KICKINIT then use noacu.c

	Now run:
	rebuild.sh
	(or rebuild.sh -n if chicken).
	On some sites you may get compilation errors if your include files
	expect an ordering different from that supplied.  For example some
	sites insist that the file <sys/types.h> is included in both pk1.c and
	pk0.c even though most systems insist is shouldn't be there.  If 
	errors occur in this area you may have to change the source to match
	your system.

3. WAIT UNTIL THE UUCP SYSTEM IS IDLE!! Single-user is best.
	su root  (it is important that chmod, chown and chgrp work below)

4. If you are currently running uucp, save the old programs!:
	cd /usr/bin
	# remember to save any other uucp programs here
	for i in uu*	     (csh: foreach i (uu*) )
	do
		mv  $i $i.old
	done					(csh: end )
	cd /usr/lib
	mv uucp uucp.old
	cd /usr/spool
	mv uucp uucp.old

5. Make new subdirectories:
	For safety:  cd /usr/spool/uucp.old; tar c .   (save Qed files on tape)
	make mkdirs
		If your system is duke, then the subdirectories created are
		named C., D., D.duke, D.dukeX, TM., XTMP and X..
	This will also create and fill in the L.dirs file, which you may
	wish to update by running addpref.sh.

6. Install the new commands:
	make install
	(If you are chicken, type 'make -n install' first).

7. Edit and install the control files:
	THESE FILES MUST BE OWNED BY THE SAME OWNER AND GROUP AS
	THE UUCP COMMANDS AND UUCP SPOOL FILES!!! (probably uucp, uucp).

	The L.dirs file will have been created by step 5, you may wish to
	remove this (if you are not running with the UUDIR/SUBCS code), or
	you may wish to extend it by adding directories for particular sites.
	If you add directories become superuser and run the addpref.sh command
	which will prompt you at each stage. It is best to do this after step 8.

	Most of the L.sys changes (if you had one in the
	old format) can be made automatically. 
	In SUPPORT:
	cc convLsys.c ../uulib.a
	and run
	a.out < /usr/lib/uucp.old/L.sys > /usr/lib/uucp/L.sys
	chown, chgrp and chmod the new L.sys.
	Then go in and check it by hand to make sure that it is all correct.
	The width field selects which version of the datawidth code to use
	if you are attempting to connect over PSS/JANET then set this field to
	7fN and make sure that the login name you use on the remote system
	calls uucico.7
	For any line connection which you do not want the FIO protocol on
	add the string "fN" to the end of the width entry, without this
	the uucico will fail to talk to the remote end.

	The format for dialers is slightly different so that any dialer
	can be handled.  Each entry in L-devices is:
		Caller Line Useful Class Dialer
	for example
		# The following lines are hard-wired
		#
		DIR tty03 unused 2400 pad
		DIR tty04 unused 2400 pad
		#
		ACU tty00 unused 300 hayes
	A DIR line is tried if the Dialer field from L-devices matches
	the phone-num field from L.sys and the speeds match too.
		eg: an L.sys line of:
		twit Any DIR 2400 8fN pad \r\r
	would first match the DIR entry for tty03 if that was busy then
	tty04 would be tried.
	An ACU line is tried if the speeds match.

	To have a line kicked (shutdown/startup getty on a line) it must
	have an entry in the /usr/lib/uucp/kickthese file.  Entries are
	one per line fixed format: ttyXY. If the Y char is a '.' it is
	treated as null for names like "tty6" (tty6.) .

	Finally there is the recently added L.info file which allows some
	degree of tailoring of the uucp system.  A minimal file will just
	say
		debuggers 0-20
		acct off
	which will allow users whose id's are in the range 0-20 to
	run uucico with the debugging flag turned on and also
	(acct off) will disable accounting.  You can also specify wether
	an individual host should be accounted for, wether mail details should
	be kept and if non-local what hostname should be given to the remote
	site.
		# Name to give to non-local sites.
		exthost kcl-cs
		#
		# a local machine no general (or mail accounting).
		west44 local
		#
		# a remote machine with full accounting on
		ukc accton acmail
	A number of other facilites
	can also be tailored see the example file in USR.LIB and the description
	in "implement".
	
	Look in USR.LIB for examples of these files.
	You should really have copies of all these files in your lib
	tailored to your site (with the sole exception of kickthese).
	
8. Move old Qed files:
	If you have spooled files, they must be moved into the subdirectories.
	try:
	mvq.sh /usr/spool/uucp.old /usr/spool/uucp
	NOTE: If you are not running with subdirectories then just move across
	all the old Qed files and DO NOT run mvq.sh.
	This uses find to scan the old Q for files with the D.dukeX (following
	the above example) prefix and cp them to the new Q (and subdirectory)
	then rm's the old file.

9. Test the new system
	Test by mailing a letter somewhere and back.
	If it works, the new system is probably fine.
	Otherwise, figure out what is wrong.
	Start by examining LOGFILE.  Try /usr/lib/uucp/uucico -r1 -sname -x7
	If things are no-go, you can back out the changes by restoring the
	old uu programs from their old directories and the spooled files
	from tape.
	NOTE: The subdirectories foul up a non-subdir version,
	so be sure that subdirectories exist/do not exist as appropriate.

10. Install handy SUPPORT / CRON
	Look in SUPPORT and CRON for handy other stuff.
	In particular, CRON/uu.* are
	shell scripts that can be run via cron hourly, daily, and weekly
	to keep uucp trim.  
	THEY MOST LIKELY NEED TO BE MODIFIED TO USE THEM ON YOUR OWN SYSTEM!

	uu.hourly		Makes sure sites are polled.
	uu.daily		Runs uuclean to clean up /usr/spool/uucp.
				**IT HANDLES SUBDIRECTORIES!!
				Renames LOGFILE to LOGFILE.old.
	uu.weekly		Renames SYSLOG to SYSLOG.old.
	The above scripts should be run via cron.

	Its also necessary to add a line to the system startup file to
	remove locks that have been left lying around on system crashing.
	Without such a line the locks would have to timeout before the
	devices/site they would become available again.

	uuusage, uuusage.1, uutbl
		Summarizes SYSLOG.  Very handy.  (Alan S. Watt).
		Works best if you use uucp.daily.  Slow if SYSLOG is big.

	uurate
		More detailed summary of SYSLOG.  Useful for checking
		throughput.  Modified from program provided by ittvax!swatt.
		Slow if SYSLOG is big.

	uucp.daily
		Daily shell script run at ittvax.  Manages SYSLOG files in
		a convenient form for uuusage.  Not integrated in time
		for this distribution.
	
	uukill	(Nigel Horne, Root Computers)
		A recently added utility designed to kill off all existing
		uucico's when run (via crontab) this can be used by sites
		with cheap rate night lines who don't want large bills.
		(It can also be used to kill off news feeding at a specific
		time, like when the users come in!)
		
	NOTE: ucu (the new cu program) is EXTREMELY untested and probably
		will not work.  If it does it is by chance.  In particular
		the SYS3/5 version is highly dubious.  Also in order
		to work correctly there must be public write permission on
		the spool directory for it to create locks.

11. Check uucp login names
You should have the following login names now
uucp, uucp4, uucp7  which call /usr/lib/uucp/uucico, uucico.4 and uucico.7
they should all have the same login directory of /usr/spool/uucppublic (or
whatever you have as the public directory for the uucp system).  (You may
also want to add uucp6, uucpf if necessary).
Its also a good idea to have a login name (appearing before the rest) of
UUCP that way all transfers will get stamped with this capitalised version
and a uucpman login name for maintanence.

12. Alter mail command.
If possible alter the mail command so when it calls uux it gives both the
'-gA' and '-OA' arguments.  The first forces the grade to be 'A' the second
will tell any uucico that is spawned on on behalf of the uux to only transfer
for grade 'A'.  The default for uux is to use grade 'o' and to allow all
grade in any spawned uucico.  Many sites will not process work not in grade
'A' during working hours.




